# TCP data-transfer vectors: what this stack must put on the wire while bytes
# are moving in either direction, and when it must put them there again.
#
# These lines are the SPECIFICATION. Where the implementation disagrees with a
# line here, the implementation is what changes. A line is only edited if it
# can be shown to be wrong, and then the reason is written down next to it --
# there are three such edits in `sequence-wrap` and six more (all PSH, all made
# by Task 17 after the differential found a real defect), all marked.
#
# The conventions are `tcp-handshake.vec`'s and are not restated: scenarios
# separated by `# scenario:` markers because `VectorFrames` pins every TCP
# line's four-tuple; absolute sequence numbers; our ISS 1000 and the guest's 0
# so the two sequence spaces cannot be confused; `win` stated on every expected
# line, because an omitted one decodes to `nil` and can never match a real
# segment.
#
# ## Two line forms that are not frames: `write <n>` and `close`
#
# A packetdrill script interleaves system calls with packets, and this file is
# the reason: half of what follows is the SEND side, and "the application wrote
# 300 bytes" is not something any sequence of packets can say. A retransmission
# vector, a zero-window vector and a local close all begin with an application
# call, and hiding it in the harness would put the one event that starts the
# scenario outside the file that claims to specify it -- at no stated time, so
# a reader could not tell whether the write came before or after the segment on
# the line above. Both forms are `<` lines: something entering the stack, from
# above rather than off the wire. See `VectorPacket`.
#
# ## The RTO floor is one second, and every connection here sits on it
#
# RFC 6298 section 2.4 floors the RTO at one second, and this stack keeps the
# standard floor rather than lowering it for a host-local gateway (see
# `RTTEstimator.minimumTimeout` for why: gVisor uses the standard floor, and a
# different one makes every loss-recovery comparison diverge for reasons that
# have nothing to do with correctness). Real RTT here is microseconds, so an
# unmeasured connection retransmits at exactly +1.000s and nothing shorter.
#
# That would leave RFC 6298's actual arithmetic -- Jacobson's smoothed estimate
# and variance -- pinned by unit tests alone, since every sample it could take
# lands under the floor and is clamped away. `rtt-sample-drives-the-rto` is the
# scenario that closes that hole: it drives two samples large enough that the
# floor is NOT the operative bound, and pins the resulting RTO twice, on the
# wire, by the time a retransmission appears.
#
# ## Nothing here runs long enough to expire an ARP entry, and that is deliberate
#
# An ARP cache entry lives 60 seconds, and the RTO backoff ladder (1, 2, 4, 8,
# 16, 32, 64) crosses that boundary at the sixth retransmission. Past it, the
# correct behaviour is an ARP REQUEST and no TCP segment at all -- `IPv4Protocol
# .send` throws `.noRoute` after asking, and `TCPEndpoint.emit` swallows that
# deliberately because the retransmission timer will bring the segment back.
# That is right in production and it reads as a vector failure. Every scenario
# in this file is therefore kept well inside one ARP lifetime (the longest is
# 2.220s), so no line here is quietly a statement about ARP. `tcp-close.vec`
# does cross 60 seconds, and says there how it handles it.
#
# ## PSH, and our advertised window barely moving
#
# 1. Our data segments DO carry PSH, on the last segment of each write. This
#    note used to say the opposite -- "`Sender` cuts segments with the ACK bit
#    and nothing else... PSH is advisory, so this is legal" -- and it was
#    wrong: RFC 1122 section 4.2.2.2 clause (2), carried forward by RFC 9293
#    section 3.9.1, makes it a MUST for a stack whose send call has no push
#    argument, and `TCPEndpoint.send` has none. The Task 17 differential found
#    it; `push-on-the-last-segment-of-a-write`, `write-under-a-small-window`
#    and `a-retransmission-keeps-the-push-bit` pin the rule; four `>` lines in
#    the three scenarios above them were edited and are marked.
#
#    Note what this cost: the note was written from the implementation, called
#    a defect a design decision, and made every vector line that depended on it
#    agree with the defect. That is exactly the failure mode a differential
#    against a stack nobody here wrote exists to catch.
# 2. Our advertised window is 65535 on essentially every line below. In-order
#    bytes are handed to the application synchronously and never retained, and
#    out-of-order bytes are held in a queue four times larger than the window,
#    so `min(free space, configured window)` is the configured window until
#    roughly 192 KiB is queued out of order. The figure is not a constant --
#    `Receiver.advertisedWindow` can drop it, and the never-retract floor
#    governs how fast -- but no scenario of this size can move it.


# scenario: in-order-data
#
# The base case, and the one every other scenario in this file is a deviation
# from. Bytes arrive in order; each segment is acknowledged with the sequence
# number one past its last byte; the acknowledgement is immediate and there is
# exactly one per segment.
#
# Immediate rather than delayed is a real choice and this is where it is
# written down. RFC 9293 section 3.8.6.3 permits delaying an ACK up to 500 ms,
# and this stack does not: `Receiver.accept` reports `shouldAck` for every
# segment that occupies sequence space, and `TCPEndpoint` sends it in the same
# pass. Two ACKs for two segments, at the two segments' own times, is what the
# last four lines say.
0.000 < S  0:0(0) win 65535 <mss 1460>
0.000 > S. 1000:1000(0) ack 1 win 65535 <mss 1460>
0.010 < .  1:1(0) ack 1001 win 65535
0.020 < P. 1:101(100) ack 1001 win 65535
0.020 > .  1001:1001(0) ack 101 win 65535
0.030 < P. 101:201(100) ack 1001 win 65535
0.030 > .  1001:1001(0) ack 201 win 65535


# scenario: out-of-order-data
#
# A gap in the stream. The second hundred bytes arrive first; the first hundred
# arrive after.
#
# The out-of-order segment draws an acknowledgement that has NOT moved --
# `ack 1`, the same number the handshake left RCV.NXT at. That is RFC 5681
# section 3.2's duplicate ACK, and it is the loss signal the peer's fast
# retransmit counts: acknowledging 201 here instead would tell the peer the
# missing hundred bytes had arrived, and they would never be sent again.
#
# The gap-filling segment is then acknowledged CUMULATIVELY, with 201 rather
# than 101 -- one ACK covering both segments, because RCV.NXT has run over the
# queued bytes the moment the hole in front of them closed. A stack that
# acknowledged only what the arriving segment itself carried would leave the
# peer holding data it has already delivered.
0.000 < S  0:0(0) win 65535 <mss 1460>
0.000 > S. 1000:1000(0) ack 1 win 65535 <mss 1460>
0.010 < .  1:1(0) ack 1001 win 65535
0.020 < P. 101:201(100) ack 1001 win 65535
0.020 > .  1001:1001(0) ack 1 win 65535
0.030 < P. 1:101(100) ack 1001 win 65535
0.030 > .  1001:1001(0) ack 201 win 65535


# scenario: retransmission-after-rto
#
# The application writes a hundred bytes; the peer never acknowledges them; the
# retransmission timer expires and the SAME segment goes out again.
#
# "The same" is the whole content of this vector, and every field of the second
# `>` line below is identical to the first by construction rather than by
# coincidence: same sequence number, same length, same acknowledgement, same
# window. A retransmission that moved the sequence number would present to the
# peer as new data at a position it never asked for; one that re-cut the
# segment from a different offset would leave a hole that no acknowledgement
# could ever fill.
#
# +1.000s exactly. Nothing has been measured on this connection, so RFC 6298
# section 2.1's initial RTO of one second stands, and the floor would hold it
# there in any case (see the header). The runner compares emission time
# exactly, with no tolerance, so a stack that fired early -- or that emitted
# the right bytes at some other moment -- fails this line rather than passing
# on content alone.
0.000 < S     0:0(0) win 65535 <mss 1460>
0.000 > S.    1000:1000(0) ack 1 win 65535 <mss 1460>
0.010 < .     1:1(0) ack 1001 win 65535
0.020 < write 100
# `P.`, not `.`. EDITED by Task 17 after the differential found this stack
# setting no PSH at all; see `push-on-the-last-segment-of-a-write` below for
# the RFC 1122 section 4.2.2.2 MUST and the rule. A hundred bytes is one
# segment and it empties the write, so it pushes -- and so does the
# retransmission, which reproduces the segment rather than re-deriving it.
0.020 > P.    1001:1101(100) ack 1 win 65535
1.020 > P.    1001:1101(100) ack 1 win 65535
# Acknowledged at last: the retransmit timer is cancelled and the scenario ends
# with nothing further on the wire. The runner reports any frame this file does
# not account for, so a stack that retransmitted a third time -- or that failed
# to cancel and fired again at +2.000s -- would be caught by the file ENDING
# here rather than by any line in it.
1.030 < .     1:1(0) ack 1101 win 65535


# scenario: zero-window
#
# The peer closes its receive window in the handshake's third leg. The
# application writes anyway, and NOTHING goes on the wire: `Sender
# .segmentsToTransmit` sends `min(cwnd, SND.WND)` bytes and SND.WND is zero.
# There is no expected frame between the write and the window update below, and
# that silence is checked -- the runner fails on any frame the script does not
# account for.
#
# The window then reopens on a bare acknowledgement that advances nothing. RFC
# 9293 section 3.10.7.4 admits SEG.ACK == SND.UNA into the acceptable-ACK
# window for exactly this reason: it retires no data, but it carries a window
# update, and a sender with no way to act on one can never leave a zero window.
#
# What this stack does NOT have, stated here because this is the scenario where
# its absence lives: a persist timer. RFC 9293 section 3.8.6.1 requires a
# zero-window probe, and `Sender` has none, so a window update that is lost on
# the way here wedges the connection until the peer sends something else. The
# vector pins what the stack does; it is not a claim that nothing is missing.
0.000 < S     0:0(0) win 65535 <mss 1460>
0.000 > S.    1000:1000(0) ack 1 win 65535 <mss 1460>
0.010 < .     1:1(0) ack 1001 win 0
0.020 < write 100
0.100 < .     1:1(0) ack 1001 win 1000
# `P.` for the same reason as `retransmission-after-rto` above: one segment,
# and it empties the write. EDITED by Task 17.
0.100 > P.    1001:1101(100) ack 1 win 65535
0.110 < .     1:1(0) ack 1101 win 1000


# scenario: sequence-wrap
#
# Sequence numbers that wrap during the connection. Everything here is correct
# only if comparison is SERIAL arithmetic (RFC 1982) rather than integer order:
# 4294967201 + 95 is 2^32, which is 0, and every test in the receive path --
# acceptability, in-window, the reassembler's offsets -- has to agree that 0 is
# ninety-five AHEAD of 4294967201 and not four billion behind it.
#
# ## Three edits to the version this scenario was drafted as
#
# 1. The 95-byte segment is written `4294967201:0(95)`, not
#    `4294967201:4294967296(95)`. 2^32 is not expressible in the 32-bit field
#    the DSL parses into, and `VectorScript` checks `end - start == length`
#    with WRAPPING arithmetic -- so `0` is not a shorthand here, it is the
#    number that is actually on the wire, and writing it out is the same claim
#    the line is about.
#
# 2. The acknowledgements below advertise `win 65535`, not the 65440 and 65340
#    they were drafted with. Those figures assume a receiver that holds
#    delivered bytes in a socket buffer until the application reads them, and
#    this stack has no such buffer and no `read`: `TCPEndpoint` hands in-order
#    bytes to `onData` synchronously and `TCPReassembler` never queues them at
#    all, so the space available after 95 bytes have been delivered is exactly
#    the space available before. Advertising 65440 would be advertising less
#    than the connection is genuinely prepared to accept. The consequence of
#    that interface is worth stating plainly rather than only implying: there
#    is no receive-side backpressure in this stack, and an application that
#    ignores `onData` is acknowledged and dropped rather than throttled.
#
# 3. A segment arrives OUT OF ORDER from the far side of the wrap, and this is
#    the edit that gives the scenario its teeth. The drafted block moved only
#    in order, and every comparison it made was between two numbers on the same
#    side of the boundary -- RCV.NXT crossed 2^32 by ADDITION, which wraps
#    whether or not comparison is serial. Replacing the serial `-` with an
#    integer difference left every line of it passing. A wrap vector has to
#    make the stack COMPARE across the boundary, not merely add across it.
0.000 < S  4294967200:4294967200(0) win 65535
0.000 > S. 1000:1000(0) ack 4294967201 win 65535 <mss 1460>
0.010 < .  4294967201:4294967201(0) ack 1001 win 512
# Fifty bytes at 0, out of order: RCV.NXT is 4294967201, so these sit 95 ahead
# of it. Under integer order they read as four billion BEHIND, the segment is
# rejected as unacceptable, and the reply is the same duplicate ACK either way
# -- an unacceptable segment and an out-of-order one both draw an ACK at
# RCV.NXT. This line alone therefore proves nothing, and the next pair is where
# the difference surfaces.
0.020 < P. 0:50(50) ack 1001 win 512
0.020 > .  1001:1001(0) ack 4294967201 win 65535
# Ninety-five bytes ending exactly at 2^32. In order, so it is delivered, and
# it closes the gap in front of the fifty already queued -- so RCV.NXT runs
# over both and lands at 50. A stack that refused the queued run above has only
# ninety-five bytes to deliver here and acknowledges 0 instead of 50, which is
# the wrap arithmetic failing where it can be seen.
0.030 < P. 4294967201:0(95) ack 1001 win 512
0.030 > .  1001:1001(0) ack 50 win 65535


# scenario: rtt-sample-drives-the-rto
#
# The one scenario in this plan where RFC 6298's estimator is the thing being
# measured rather than a floor being observed. The peer's window is held at 100
# bytes so each acknowledgement releases exactly one more segment, which makes
# every round trip a clean, unambiguous sample under Karn's algorithm.
#
# Sample 1 is 500 ms (sent 0.010, acknowledged 0.510). RFC 6298 section 2.2,
# the first-sample rule: SRTT = R = 500 ms, RTTVAR = R/2 = 250 ms, and
# RTO = SRTT + max(G, 4*RTTVAR) = 500 ms + 1000 ms = 1.500 s. Above the floor,
# so the floor is not what decides it.
#
# Sample 2 is 100 ms (sent 0.510, acknowledged 0.610). RFC 6298 section 2.3 in
# Jacobson's fixed-point form, which is what `RTTEstimator` implements and what
# every stack this one is compared against implements:
#
#   RTTVAR = RTTVAR - (RTTVAR >> 2) + (|SRTT - R| >> 2)
#          = 250 - 62.5 + 100 = 287.5 ms
#   SRTT   = SRTT - (SRTT >> 3) + (R >> 3)
#          = 500 - 62.5 + 12.5 = 450 ms
#   RTO    = 450 + max(1, 4 * 287.5) = 1.600 s
#
# The third segment goes out at 0.610 and is never acknowledged, so the RTO is
# read off the wire as the gap to its retransmission: 0.610 + 1.600 = 2.210.
# One millisecond either side is a different SRTT or a different RTTVAR, and
# `3/4 * RTTVAR` computed with integer division instead of the shift form
# differs in the low bits -- which is exactly the divergence the shift form was
# chosen to avoid, made visible here as a retransmission at the wrong time.
0.000 < S     0:0(0) win 65535 <mss 1460>
0.000 > S.    1000:1000(0) ack 1 win 65535 <mss 1460>
0.010 < .     1:1(0) ack 1001 win 100
0.010 < write 300
# `P.` on all three pieces, EDITED by Task 17. Three hundred bytes is less than
# one MSS, so this write is split by the peer's hundred-byte WINDOW and not by
# the MSS -- and a window split does not defer the push. This is the same rule
# `write-under-a-small-window` below exists to pin, met here by accident, which
# is why that scenario states it deliberately rather than relying on this one.
0.010 > P.    1001:1101(100) ack 1 win 65535
0.510 < .     1:1(0) ack 1101 win 100
0.510 > P.    1101:1201(100) ack 1 win 65535
0.610 < .     1:1(0) ack 1201 win 100
0.610 > P.    1201:1301(100) ack 1 win 65535
# 0.610 + 1.600. Not 0.610 + 1.000: the floor has been left behind, and a stack
# that ignored its own measurements would retransmit at 1.610 instead.
2.210 > P.    1201:1301(100) ack 1 win 65535
2.220 < .     1:1(0) ack 1301 win 100


# scenario: push-on-the-last-segment-of-a-write
#
# FOUND BY THE DIFFERENTIAL (Task 17), seed 6840123409045651459, step 4. This
# stack cut every data segment with the ACK bit and nothing else; gVisor set
# PSH on the last segment of each write. That is not a matter of taste:
#
#   RFC 1122 section 4.2.2.2, carried forward by RFC 9293 section 3.9.1 --
#   "A TCP MAY implement PUSH flags on SEND calls. If PUSH flags are not
#   implemented, then the sending TCP: (1) must not buffer data indefinitely,
#   and (2) MUST set the PSH bit in the last buffered segment (i.e., when there
#   is no more queued data to be sent)."
#
# `TCPEndpoint.send` has no push argument, so clause (2) applies unconditionally
# and this stack was violating a MUST on every write it ever made. gVisor is
# right and we were wrong; the header note that used to say "our data lines are
# `.` and never `P.`" was recording a defect as a design decision.
#
# The rule implemented, which is Linux's and gVisor's: a segment carries PSH
# exactly when the bytes remaining in the WRITE it is cut from, counted from
# that segment's first byte, are no more than one MSS -- that is, when nothing
# more of that write will follow it as a full segment. A write is split at the
# MSS and at the peer's window, and the two splits behave differently on
# purpose (gVisor `snd.go`'s `splitSeg`, Linux `tcp_write_xmit`).
#
# Three lines pin the three cases at once. The MSS here is 1460 (the guest
# offered `mss 1460` and the link carries 1500), and the write is 2941 bytes:
# 1460 + 1460 + 21. The first two segments have more than an MSS of their write
# still to come, so they carry no PSH; the last one empties the write and does.
# A stack that set PSH on every segment fails the first line; one that set it
# on none fails the third; one that set it only when the whole send queue
# drained would pass here and fail `write-under-a-small-window` below.
0.000 < S     0:0(0) win 65535 <mss 1460>
0.000 > S.    1000:1000(0) ack 1 win 65535 <mss 1460>
0.010 < .     1:1(0) ack 1001 win 65535
0.020 < write 2941
0.020 > .     1001:2461(1460) ack 1 win 65535
0.020 > .     2461:3921(1460) ack 1 win 65535
0.020 > P.    3921:3942(21) ack 1 win 65535
0.030 < .     1:1(0) ack 3942 win 65535


# scenario: write-under-a-small-window
#
# The other half of the PSH rule, and the reason it is stated in terms of the
# write rather than of the send queue. The write is 1000 bytes -- less than one
# MSS -- and the peer's window admits only 400 at a time, so it goes out in
# three pieces. Every one of them carries PSH, because at each cut the whole
# remainder of the write was already inside one segment's worth: the split is
# the window's doing, not the MSS's, and a window split does not defer the
# push.
#
# This is the line that separates the rule from the simpler "PSH when the send
# queue empties": that rule would put PSH on the third segment alone.
0.000 < S     0:0(0) win 400 <mss 1460>
0.000 > S.    1000:1000(0) ack 1 win 65535 <mss 1460>
0.010 < .     1:1(0) ack 1001 win 400
0.020 < write 1000
0.020 > P.    1001:1401(400) ack 1 win 65535
0.030 < .     1:1(0) ack 1401 win 400
0.030 > P.    1401:1801(400) ack 1 win 65535
0.040 < .     1:1(0) ack 1801 win 400
0.040 > P.    1801:2001(200) ack 1 win 65535
0.050 < .     1:1(0) ack 2001 win 400


# scenario: a-retransmission-keeps-the-push-bit
#
# A retransmission reproduces the segment it lost, and PSH is part of that
# segment. gVisor re-sends the segment object with its flags intact; a stack
# that rebuilt the segment from the byte queue and re-derived the flags would
# get this right only by accident, and `Sender.retransmitOldest` did exactly
# that -- it hardcoded `.ack`.
#
# The write is 100 bytes, so its one segment carries PSH. At +1.000s, so does
# the retransmission.
0.000 < S     0:0(0) win 65535 <mss 1460>
0.000 > S.    1000:1000(0) ack 1 win 65535 <mss 1460>
0.010 < .     1:1(0) ack 1001 win 65535
0.020 < write 100
0.020 > P.    1001:1101(100) ack 1 win 65535
1.020 > P.    1001:1101(100) ack 1 win 65535
1.030 < .     1:1(0) ack 1101 win 65535


# scenario: window-updates-are-not-duplicate-acknowledgements
#
# FOUND BY THE DIFFERENTIAL (Task 17), seed 6840123409045651459, step 12. This
# stack fast-retransmitted a segment nothing had been lost of, because it read
# RFC 5681 section 3.2's condition (e) off the wrong variable.
#
# (e) is "the advertised window in the incoming acknowledgment equals the
# advertised window in the LAST INCOMING ACKNOWLEDGMENT" -- a comparison
# between two numbers that arrived on the wire. `Sender.acknowledged` compared
# `tcb.sndWnd` before and after instead, which is the window AFTER RFC 9293
# section 3.10.7.4's update rule has decided whether to take it. Those two are
# not the same thing, and the gap between them is reachable by the peer:
#
#   the update rule refuses a window carried by a segment whose SND.WL1 is not
#   newer, so once ONE out-of-order segment has pushed SND.WL1 forward, every
#   later acknowledgement at a lower sequence number leaves SND.WND frozen --
#   and a frozen SND.WND reads as "the window did not change" no matter what
#   the peer actually advertised.
#
# So a guest that sends one segment out of order and then three bare
# acknowledgements with three DIFFERENT windows makes this stack halve its
# congestion window and retransmit. Nothing was lost. gVisor gets this right
# (`snd.go`'s `isDupAck` compares `s.SndWnd` against `seg.window`, and stashes
# `s.SndWnd = rcvdSeg.window` only afterwards, so its stored value is exactly
# "the window in the last incoming acknowledgement").
#
# The out-of-order segment at 5001 is not decoration: it is what makes SND.WL1
# unreachable and is the only reason the three window updates can be mistaken
# for duplicates at all. The file ENDING after the last of them is what pins
# the absence of the retransmission -- the runner fails on any frame a script
# does not account for.
0.000 < S     0:0(0) win 65535 <mss 1460>
0.000 > S.    1000:1000(0) ack 1 win 65535 <mss 1460>
0.010 < .     1:1(0) ack 1001 win 65535
0.020 < write 100
0.020 > P.    1001:1101(100) ack 1 win 65535
# Out of order: queued, not delivered, and acknowledged with a duplicate ACK
# naming RCV.NXT. SND.WL1 is now 5001.
0.030 < .     5001:5101(100) ack 1001 win 65535
0.030 > .     1101:1101(0) ack 1 win 65535
# Three window updates, three different windows, all naming SND.UNA. Not one
# of them is a duplicate acknowledgement.
0.040 < .     1:1(0) ack 1001 win 40000
0.050 < .     1:1(0) ack 1001 win 30000
0.060 < .     1:1(0) ack 1001 win 20000


# scenario: three-identical-duplicate-acknowledgements-still-fast-retransmit
#
# The positive control for the scenario above, and the reason it is a separate
# vector rather than a comment: a stack that fixed the window test by never
# counting duplicates at all would pass `window-updates-are-not-duplicate-
# acknowledgements` perfectly.
#
# Same shape, same out-of-order segment, same three bare acknowledgements --
# but all four advertise the SAME window -- the out-of-order segment included,
# because RFC 5681 section 3.2 condition (e) compares against "the last
# incoming acknowledgment" and a data segment carrying an ACK is one. So all
# three bare acknowledgements are duplicates, and the third is the loss signal
# that retransmits the oldest unacknowledged segment (section 3.2 step 3). At
# 0.060, on the third, not the second and not the fourth.
#
# Note what changing ONE number does: give the first bare acknowledgement a
# different window from the out-of-order segment and the count restarts, so
# only two duplicates accrue and nothing is retransmitted. That is the whole
# difference between this scenario and the one above it.
0.000 < S     0:0(0) win 65535 <mss 1460>
0.000 > S.    1000:1000(0) ack 1 win 65535 <mss 1460>
0.010 < .     1:1(0) ack 1001 win 65535
0.020 < write 100
0.020 > P.    1001:1101(100) ack 1 win 65535
0.030 < .     5001:5101(100) ack 1001 win 65535
0.030 > .     1101:1101(0) ack 1 win 65535
0.040 < .     1:1(0) ack 1001 win 65535
0.050 < .     1:1(0) ack 1001 win 65535
0.060 < .     1:1(0) ack 1001 win 65535
0.060 > P.    1001:1101(100) ack 1 win 65535


# scenario: a-timeout-keeps-retransmitting
#
# FOUND BY THE DIFFERENTIAL against gVisor. After a retransmission timeout this
# stack sent the earliest unacknowledged segment and then NOTHING more until the
# next expiry. RFC 6298 section 5.4 asks for no more than that, so the stack was
# conformant and unusable at once: 5.4 does not say to STOP there, and gVisor and
# Linux go on retransmitting the following segments as acknowledgements arrive.
# On the 1, 2, 4, 8, 16 s ladder this five-segment burst cost 31 seconds here
# against roughly one anywhere else.
#
# Five 1460-byte segments go out and every one of them is lost -- the guest
# acknowledges none of them, which is why nothing follows the write until the
# timer expires at +1.000s.
#
# What each line after the expiry is: the expiry itself sends 1001:2461 and
# presumes the other four lost, so cwnd (RFC 5681 section 3.1: one segment, 1460)
# is exactly full and nothing else may go. Every acknowledgement after that
# retires a segment, grows cwnd by one segment in slow start, and the room that
# opens is spent on the segments still missing, IN SEQUENCE, before any new data:
#
#   1.030  cwnd 2920  ->  two segments, 2461:3921 and 3921:5381
#   1.040  cwnd 4380  ->  two segments, 5381:6841 and 6841:8301
#
# So the whole burst is back on the wire 20ms after the first acknowledgement,
# on ONE expiry. The old behaviour needed four more -- at 3.020, 7.020, 15.020
# and 31.020 -- and this file ending where it does is what says none of them
# happened: the runner reports any frame the script does not account for.
#
# The lines are also the vacuity check the unit tests state directly. A sender
# that answered the timeout by putting all five segments back at 1.020, ignoring
# the congestion window, would recover in one round trip and fail this scenario
# on its very first line after the expiry.
#
# PSH sits on 6841:8301 and nowhere else, by `push-on-the-last-segment-of-a-
# write` above: the write is exactly five segments, so only the fifth empties it.
# The retransmissions reproduce the segments they lost, PSH included.
0.000 < S     0:0(0) win 65535 <mss 1460>
0.000 > S.    1000:1000(0) ack 1 win 65535 <mss 1460>
0.010 < .     1:1(0) ack 1001 win 65535
0.020 < write 7300
0.020 > .     1001:2461(1460) ack 1 win 65535
0.020 > .     2461:3921(1460) ack 1 win 65535
0.020 > .     3921:5381(1460) ack 1 win 65535
0.020 > .     5381:6841(1460) ack 1 win 65535
0.020 > P.    6841:8301(1460) ack 1 win 65535
1.020 > .     1001:2461(1460) ack 1 win 65535
1.030 < .     1:1(0) ack 2461 win 65535
1.030 > .     2461:3921(1460) ack 1 win 65535
1.030 > .     3921:5381(1460) ack 1 win 65535
1.040 < .     1:1(0) ack 3921 win 65535
1.040 > .     5381:6841(1460) ack 1 win 65535
1.040 > P.    6841:8301(1460) ack 1 win 65535
1.050 < .     1:1(0) ack 5381 win 65535
1.060 < .     1:1(0) ack 6841 win 65535
1.070 < .     1:1(0) ack 8301 win 65535
